{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And He said to him, I am HaShem.</b> From 'after these things' until here was said to him at night, as it is written 'במחזה' — meaning in a night vision, and further it is written 'and count the stars.' From here onward was said to him by day, as it is said 'and the sun was about to set.' And it is called the pericope of the Covenant Between the Pieces, and it is earlier than the pericope above — for 'after these things' is five years later. For it is written here 'for your seed shall be a stranger, etc.' — meaning from Isaac onward — 'in a land not theirs, and they shall serve them and they shall afflict them four hundred years.' And it is written 'and it was at the end of thirty years and four hundred years from the time of the Covenant Between the Pieces, all the hosts went out, etc.' If so, you find that the pericope of the Covenant Between the Pieces preceded Isaac's birth by thirty years, and Abraham was a hundred years old when Isaac his son was born to him; so Abraham was seventy years old at the time of the Covenant Between the Pieces, which was when he left Haran the first time. And the first time, when the pericope of 'after these things' was said to him, he was seventy-five years old. For we learn in Seder Olam: he was forty-eight years old at the Dispersion, and twelve years they served Kedarlaomer, and thirteen years they rebelled, and in the fourteenth year Kedarlaomer came — so from the Dispersion to the war is twenty-six years. Put these twenty-six upon the forty-eight that he had before the Dispersion, and they amount to seventy-four — so we find that at the time of the war Abram was seventy-four or seventy-five years old. And regarding this it is said 'and Abram was seventy-five years old when he went out from Haran' — the second time. Immediately after 'these things' — that he killed the kings — 'the word of HaShem came to Abram במחזה saying, etc.' But there is no earlier and later in the Torah, and the pericope of the Covenant Between the Pieces was placed here in order to connect the pericopes in which Abraham was promised seed. And this is what we learned in Seder Olam: he returned to Haran after the Covenant Between the Pieces, which was in the Land of Israel, as it is written in the pericope of the Covenant Between the Pieces, 'to your seed I have given this land.' And he lingered there in Haran five years, and at that time he was seventy-five years old, and he went out from Haran a second time. <b>Another proof.</b> For they are two separate matters, as is stated in Berakhot: from the day the Holy One, blessed be He, created His world, there was no person who called the Holy One, blessed be He, 'Adon' — until Abraham came and called Him Adon, as it is said, 'HaShem Elohim, במה אדע כי אירשנה.' And if you should say it is one matter — from 'after these things' until 'and Sarai the wife of Abram' — why does the Talmud not cite 'HaShem Elohim, מה תתן לי,' which precedes it? Rather, it is obvious: they are two pericopes, and the pericope of the Covenant Between the Pieces is earlier than what precedes it by five years, as I explained above. And further, if the order of these pericopes is as written, how could Abraham say 'במה אדע כי אירשנה'? There is no smaller person of faith who would say 'במה אדע כי אירשנה' after the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him 'he who shall come forth from your innards, he shall inherit you.' Rather, it is obvious: they are two pericopes, and the latter preceded what is before it, as we say. And now it works out: the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him, 'I brought you out of Ur of the Chaldees to give you this land to inherit it.' Abraham said before the Holy One, blessed be He: since I have no seed, במה אדע כי אירשנה — perhaps today or tomorrow I shall die, and how shall I inherit it? <b>One may say:</b> according to the method of Rashi's commentary, Abraham did not ask Him for a sign regarding the promise to give him the land; rather, he was worried about this promise, for all the promises of the Holy One, blessed be He, are conditional — if they fulfill His commandments. As we find with the wilderness generation: 'and He swore saying, if these men shall see, etc.' Therefore he asked the Holy One, blessed be He, for a covenant, for His oaths are unconditional. And if you ask: according to the one who says he did not ask Him for a sign, but asked by what merit they would endure in it — if so, why was he punished with 'ידע תדע'? One may answer that this is not a punishment, but rather He thus answered him: by the merit of the sacrifices and the exile they will endure in it. Here Rashi explained: by what merit will they endure in it? He said to him: by the merit of the sacrifices."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The closing line 'כאן פרש״י…' appears to be a copyist's marginal annotation rather than Chizkuni's own words, but it is rendered as part of the continuous text as given."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:04:32.291451264Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}